  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nExpect fog and stratus to develop this morning with low level\nmoisture in place. Conditions should improve this morning as\nmixing begins. A shortwave trough, and inverted surface trough,\nare expected to cross the region this afternoon and early\nevening. Expect scattered showers and thunderstorms to develop\nagain today, especially in the afternoon. PWATs are progged to\nbe lower today than on recent days, lowering into the 1.4 to 1.6\ninch range by late afternoon. Model soundings also indicate some\ndry advection aloft. Current probabilities keep the highest\nprobability for 1 inch or more of rain to the east of the area,\nwhich is similar to the latest slight risk of excessive rainfall\nfrom WPC. At this time, decided to not issue a flash flood \nwatch for today with more uncertainty in the coverage and extent\nof any convection today. \n\nThe potential for severe weather appears low today as well.\nOverall flow is weak, though with dry air aloft, gusty \nthunderstorms will be possible.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nConditions are expected to be less favorable for convection on\nWednesday, with flat ridging and a possible cap aloft. Expect\nthe best chance for convection across the higher terrain areas,\nthough outflows and low convective temperatures could result in\na shower or storm outside of the terrain as well. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\nA series of shortwaves are expected to cross the region\nThursday, continuing into the weekend. This will keep periodic\nshower and thunderstorms in the forecast. The latest ML guidance\nindicates a more favorable potential for severe storms with the\nThursday shortwave, especially if some dry mid level air moves \nacross the area during the afternoon as some models indicate. \nAt least some potential for severe storms and locally heavy \nrainfall will continue into the weekend, with diurnally driven \ninstability and some increase in shear. Locally heavy rainfall\nwill remain a concern with lower flash flood guidance values due\nto the recent heavy rainfall.",
